Month = {AUG 7},
Abstract = {Early detection and prevention of psychiatric disorders, particularly
   depression, remain as major global health challenges, yet reliable tools
   for identifying individuals before symptom onset are lacking. Here, we
   combine functional neuroimaging with computational modeling to identify
   a mechanistic biomarker of depression risk. In a population-based
   adolescent cohort (IMAGEN, N = 1332), we found that weakened neural
   representations of emotional signals were linked to depressive symptoms.
   Perturbation experiments in a brain-aligned deep learning model showed
   that this deficit reflects overregularized emotion perception, producing
   a negative perceptual bias. A neurocomputational signature of this
   mechanism predicted depression symptom onset up to 4 years later at the
   IMAGEN follow-up (N = 725), was associated with both a genetic-risk
   variant and polygenic risk for depression, and improved depression
   classification in a patient cohort (STRATIFY, N = 411). These findings
   suggest a possible mechanism linking genetic vulnerability to altered
   emotion perception and future depression, and propose a predictive
   computational marker with potential for early detection and prevention.},
